  "image_name": "70b3d72c5167051020c7b2954ad5fb9f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a simplified phosphorus system linking mining, agriculture, food use, waste handling, and environmental pollution, while also highlighting weak coordination among stakeholders. The main block shows phosphorus entering from phosphate rock mining and the fertilizer industry, emphasizing dependence on imported mineral resources. Fertilizer flows to crop farmers, then into crops, livestock, and food consumed by households. Some phosphorus is recycled through manure and sewage sludge back to fields, but these recycled amounts appear modest compared with the main agricultural flows.\n\nA central message is that phosphorus moves through the food system inefficiently: crop residues, manure, food crops, food-livestock transfers, wastewater, and food waste are all shown as interconnected pathways, with some phosphorus stored as “soil legacy P.” Losses from agriculture and waste streams reach water bodies through leaching, runoff, wastewater discharge, and sediments, leading to eutrophication.\n\nThe lower block adds a governance dimension. Academia, government, and legislators are shown separately, with dashed lines interrupted by red crosses, suggesting fragmented management and poor collaboration across sectors. Overall, the image argues that phosphorus use is shaped both by material flows and by institutional disconnection, which together contribute to waste and water pollution.",
